.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
.TH GWPROXY 1 "July 2026" "gwproxy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gwproxy \- fast multi-threaded TCP, SOCKS5, HTTP and HTTPS proxy for GNU/Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gwproxy
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gwproxy
(the GNU/Weeb Proxy) is a fast, multi-threaded TCP proxy for GNU/Linux. A
single instance can act as any combination of:
.IP \(bu 3
a plain TCP\-to\-TCP forwarder to a fixed
.BR \-\-target ;
.IP \(bu 3
a
.B SOCKS5
proxy (RFC 1928) with CONNECT to IPv4, IPv6 and domain\-name targets, UDP
ASSOCIATE relaying to IPv4/IPv6 targets (domain\-name targets and upstream\-proxy
chaining are not supported for UDP), and optional username/password
authentication (RFC 1929);
.IP \(bu 3
an
.B HTTP
proxy supporting both CONNECT tunnelling and forwarding of ordinary
absolute\-form requests ("GET http://host/path"), with optional "Basic" proxy
authentication (RFC 7617);
.IP \(bu 3
an
.B HTTPS
proxy that terminates the client's TLS on the listener (see
.B TLS TERMINATION
below), when built with OpenSSL support;
.IP \(bu 3
a
.B transparent
proxy that recovers each connection's original destination from
.BR SO_ORIGINAL_DST .
.PP
SOCKS5 and HTTP can be enabled together on one listening port; the protocol is
auto\-detected per connection from the client's first bytes, and both share a
single credential store. When TLS termination is enabled, a TLS ClientHello is
likewise auto\-detected, so plaintext and TLS clients coexist on the same port.
.PP
gwproxy runs a configurable number of worker threads that each accept
connections through
.B SO_REUSEPORT
and drive I/O with either the
.B epoll
(default) or
.B io_uring
event loop. It recovers gracefully from file\-descriptor exhaustion
(EMFILE/ENFILE), caches DNS answers for hostname targets, and can mark its own
outgoing connections with
.BR SO_MARK .
.SH OPTIONS
Every boolean option takes an explicit
.B 0
or
.B 1
argument. Long options and their single\-letter equivalents are interchangeable.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-event\-loop=\fIname\fR
Event loop to use:
.B epoll
or
.BR io_uring .
The
.B io_uring
loop is only available when gwproxy was built with io_uring support (see
.BR "AVAILABILITY" ).
Default:
.BR epoll .
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bind=\fIaddr:port\fR
Address and port to listen on. IPv6 literals are written in brackets, e.g.
.BR [::1]:1080 " or " 0.0.0.0:8080 .
The default,
.BR [::]:1080 ,
listens on the dual\-stack IPv4/IPv6 wildcard.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target=\fIaddr:port\fR
For plain TCP forwarding, the fixed destination every accepted connection is
proxied to. Ignored in SOCKS5/HTTP/transparent modes, where the destination
comes from the protocol or the socket.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-nr\-workers=\fInr\fR
Number of worker threads, each with its own
.B SO_REUSEPORT
listener. Default:
.BR 4 .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-protocol\-timeout=\fIsec\fR
Seconds allowed for a client to complete the SOCKS5/HTTP (and, if enabled, TLS)
handshake before the connection is dropped. Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-connect\-timeout=\fIsec\fR
Seconds allowed to establish the connection to the target (or upstream proxy)
before giving up. This bounds the whole attempt, including every address tried
for the same name. Default:
.BR 5 .
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-connect\-attempt\-delay=\fIms\fR
Milliseconds to wait before racing the next address of a multi\-homed target,
as in Happy Eyeballs (RFC 8305 Section 5). When a name resolves to several
addresses gwproxy starts the first, and if it has neither succeeded nor failed
within this delay it starts the next one alongside it; the first connection to
succeed wins and the others are dropped. This is what keeps a black\-holed
address cheap: without racing such an address costs the whole
.B \-\-connect\-timeout
before anything else is tried, because nothing ever reports a failure. Set to
.B 0
to disable racing, leaving only sequential fallback, which still steps to the
next address when one actively fails (refused, unreachable). Ignored when an
upstream proxy is configured, since every attempt would dial the same proxy.
Default:
.BR 250 .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid\-file=\fIfile\fR
Write the process ID to
.IR file .
Not written by default.
.SS Proxy mode selection
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-as\-socks5=\fI0|1\fR
Run as a SOCKS5 proxy. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-as\-http=\fI0|1\fR
Run as an HTTP proxy (CONNECT and forwarding). Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-udp\-associate=\fI0|1\fR
Allow the SOCKS5
.B UDP ASSOCIATE
command. When
.BR 0 ,
the proxy rejects it with reply
.B 0x07
(command not supported), leaving TCP
.B CONNECT
unaffected; only meaningful with
.BR \-\-as\-socks5 .
Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-as\-transparent=\fI0|1\fR
Run as a transparent proxy, taking the target from
.B SO_ORIGINAL_DST
(see
.BR "TRANSPARENT PROXY" ).
Cannot be combined with
.B \-\-as\-socks5
or
.BR \-\-as\-http .
Default:
.BR 0 .
.PP
.RS
If none of the above is set and
.B \-\-target
is given, gwproxy is a plain TCP forwarder.
.B \-\-as\-socks5
and
.B \-\-as\-http
may be set together to serve both protocols on one port.
.RE
.SS Authentication
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-auth\-file=\fIfile\fR
File of
.B username:password
credentials, one per line, shared by SOCKS5 (RFC 1929) and HTTP "Basic" (RFC
7617) authentication. When set, clients of either protocol must present valid
credentials. The file is watched and hot\-reloaded on change. Without it, no
authentication is required (see
.BR "AUTHENTICATION" ).
.SS Access control
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-acl\-file=\fIfile\fR
Load an iptables\-style access\-control list that filters incoming clients
.RB ( INPUT " chain)"
and outgoing targets
.RB ( OUTPUT " chain)."
The file is watched and hot\-reloaded on change, and a malformed file is
rejected (at startup, or by keeping the previous rules on reload). When
.B \-\-acl\-file
is not given, a built\-in default ACL is applied instead (see
.BR \-\-acl\-allow\-all
below). Each line is one directive, evaluated top\-to\-bottom
with first\-match\-wins; a chain's policy applies when nothing matches:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.BI "\-P " "chain target"
\(em set a chain's default policy
.RB ( ACCEPT " or " REJECT ).
.IP \(bu 3
.BI "\-A " "chain [matches] " "\fB\-j\fR " target
\(em append a rule. Matches, each negatable with a leading
.BR ! :
.BR \-s / \-\-source " and " \-d / \-\-destination " (" IP/CIDR ", "
.B \-d
is
.BR OUTPUT "-only), " "\-m domain \-\-domain" " (" OUTPUT "-only, target"
hostname; or
.B \-\-domain\-regexp
for a PCRE match, see below),
.BR "\-m user \-\-user" " (" OUTPUT "-only, the authenticated username; or"
.B \-\-user\-regexp
for a PCRE match),
.BR \-p / \-\-protocol " (" tcp / udp "), " \-\-sports " and " \-\-dports
(a port, a
.IR lo \- hi
range, or a comma list;
.B \-\-dports
is
.BR OUTPUT "-only). The target is " ACCEPT ", " REJECT ", " DNAT
.RB ( OUTPUT "-only) with " \-\-to\-destination " " \fIaddr\fR [ :\fIport\fR ],
which rewrites the destination (address and/or port, either optional) and, like
iptables, is terminal \(em it accepts the connection and stops matching (DNAT
applies to SOCKS5, HTTP, plain and transparent forwarding and, when chaining, to
the destination handed to an upstream proxy \(em including replacing a
.B socks5h://
hostname with the rewritten IP \(em but not the UDP relay), or
.B MARK
.RB ( OUTPUT "-only) with " \-\-set\-mark " " \fInum\fR "."
.IP
.B \-j MARK
sets the fwmark
.RB ( SO_MARK )
on the outgoing target socket, overriding the global
.BR \-\-mark ,
for policy routing / iptables matching. Unlike iptables it is a
.I composable modifier
here, not a terminal action: it records the mark and eval keeps matching, so a
connection can accumulate a mark and still fall through to a terminal
.BR ACCEPT / REJECT / DNAT
or the chain policy. The last matching
.B \-j MARK
wins. Like
.BR "\-j BIND" ,
it is
.I strict:
if setting
.B SO_MARK
fails (it needs
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN )
the connection is dropped rather than egressing unmarked via the wrong route.
It applies to TCP target connections (SOCKS5, HTTP, plain and transparent), not
the UDP relay. (The coarse global
.B \-\-mark
remains best\-effort.)
.IP
.B \-j BIND
.RB ( OUTPUT "-only) with " \-\-to\-source " " \fIaddr\fR [ :\fIport\fR ] " and/or"
.B \-\-to\-iface
.IR ifname ,
pins the outgoing connection to a source address (and/or port) and/or a network
interface \(em e.g. to route a given user's egress out of a specific WireGuard
tunnel. At least one of
.B \-\-to\-source
/
.B \-\-to\-iface
is required. Like
.BR MARK ,
it is a composable modifier (the last match wins) and applies to the same TCP
target connections. It is
.I strict:
if binding the interface
.RB ( SO_BINDTODEVICE )
or the source address
.RB ( bind (2))
fails \(em including a
.B \-\-to\-source
whose address family does not match the target \(em the connection is dropped
rather than continued on the wrong source. It generally needs
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN .
Binding a fixed source
.I port
limits concurrency to one connection per identical destination (a 4\-tuple
clash); omit the port (the default) for an ephemeral source port.
.RE
.IP
Addresses match IPv4 and its IPv4\-mapped IPv6 form equally. A rejected target
is refused (SOCKS5 reply
.BR 0x02 ,
HTTP
.BR 403 );
a rejected client connection is closed.
.IP
.B \-\-domain\-regexp
.I pattern
(an alternative to
.BR \-\-domain ,
also under
.BR "\-m domain" )
matches the requested hostname with a PCRE. It requires a build with PCRE2
support (see
.BR "AVAILABILITY" )
and is rejected otherwise. The pattern is matched
.I raw
and unanchored (case\-insensitively): it can match anywhere in the hostname
unless you anchor it yourself with
.BR ^ " and " $ .
A pattern must contain no whitespace (rules are whitespace\-tokenised).
.IP
.BR "\-m user \-\-user" " " \fIname\fR
matches the username the client authenticated as (via
.BR \-\-auth\-file );
.B \-\-user\-regexp
takes a PCRE instead (case\-sensitive, raw/unanchored, needs
.BR \-\-use\-pcre ).
It is
.B OUTPUT
\-only and matches nothing when the connection is unauthenticated, so it only
has effect together with
.BR \-\-auth\-file .
The
.BR \-\-user\-regexp
option, like
.BR \-\-domain\-regexp ,
requires a PCRE2 build.
.TP
.B \-\-acl\-allow\-all
Do not apply the built\-in default ACL; allow all targets (the behaviour before
a default ACL existed). This is ignored when
.B \-\-acl\-file
is given \(em the file's rules always apply as written.
.PP
.RS
When neither
.B \-\-acl\-file
nor
.B \-\-acl\-allow\-all
is given, gwproxy applies a built\-in default ACL that rejects outgoing
connections to loopback and private address ranges (SSRF hardening) while
accepting all clients:
.RS
.nf
\-P INPUT ACCEPT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 10.0.0.0/8 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 127.0.0.0/8 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 192.168.0.0/16 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d 172.16.0.0/12 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d fe80::/10 \-j REJECT
\-A OUTPUT \-d fc00::/7 \-j REJECT
\-P OUTPUT ACCEPT
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SS TLS termination (HTTPS proxy)
These options require a build with OpenSSL support (see
.BR "AVAILABILITY" ).
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-tls\-cert=\fIfile\fR
PEM certificate chain presented to TLS clients. Providing it (together with
.BR \-\-tls\-key )
enables TLS termination on the listener.
.TP
.BR \-Y ", " \-\-tls\-key=\fIfile\fR
PEM private key matching
.BR \-\-tls\-cert .
Both must be given together.
.SS DNS resolution
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-prefer\-ipv6=\fI0|1\fR
Prefer IPv6 addresses when resolving proxy hostname targets. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-dns\-cache\-secs=\fIsec\fR
Cache proxy DNS answers for this many seconds.
.B 0
or negative disables caching. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-nr\-dns\-workers=\fInr\fR
Number of DNS resolver worker threads. Default:
.BR 4 .
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-dns\-server=\fIaddr:port\fR
Upstream DNS server for the built\-in raw resolver (see
.BR \-\-raw\-dns ).
Requires a build with the new DNS resolver. Default:
.BR 1.1.1.1 .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-raw\-dns=\fI0|1\fR
Use the built\-in raw DNS resolver for SOCKS5/HTTP hostname targets instead of
the system resolver. Requires a build with the new DNS resolver. Default:
.BR 0 .
.SS Upstream proxy chaining
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-upstream\-proxy=\fIurl\fR
Route every outgoing connection through a next\-hop proxy. The URL scheme
selects the proxy type:
.BR socks5://[user:pass@]host:port ,
a SOCKS5 proxy that resolves target hostnames locally;
.BR socks5h://[user:pass@]host:port ,
a SOCKS5 proxy that resolves them itself; or
.BR http://[user:pass@]host:port ,
an HTTP proxy reached with an HTTP CONNECT request. Optional
.B user:pass
supplies username/password authentication to the upstream (RFC\ 1929 for
SOCKS5, HTTP Basic for HTTP). The port defaults to 1080 for SOCKS5 and 8080
for HTTP.
.SS Networking
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mark=\fInr\fR
Set
.B SO_MARK
(fwmark) on outgoing connections, for policy routing and iptables matching.
Requires
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN .
.B 0
disables it. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-target\-buf\-size=\fInr\fR
Size in bytes of the per\-connection buffer for target\(->client data. Default:
.BR 2048 .
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-client\-buf\-size=\fInr\fR
Size in bytes of the per\-connection buffer for client\(->target data. Default:
.BR 2048 .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-tcp\-nodelay=\fI0|1\fR
Enable
.B TCP_NODELAY
on connections. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-K ", " \-\-tcp\-quickack=\fI0|1\fR
Enable
.B TCP_QUICKACK
on connections. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-tcp\-keepalive=\fI0|1\fR
Enable
.B TCP
keepalive on connections. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-tcp\-keepidle=\fIsec\fR
.B TCP_KEEPIDLE
in seconds. Default:
.BR 60 .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-tcp\-keepintvl=\fIsec\fR
.B TCP_KEEPINTVL
in seconds. Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-tcp\-keepcnt=\fInr\fR
.B TCP_KEEPCNT
probe count. Default:
.BR 5 .
.SS Logging
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-log\-level=\fIlevel\fR
Verbosity:
.B 0
none,
.B 1
error,
.B 2
warning,
.B 3
info,
.B 4
debug. Default:
.BR 3 .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-log\-file=\fIfile\fR
Write logs to
.IR file .
Default:
.BR /dev/stdout .
.SH PROXY MODES
.SS Plain TCP forwarding
With no proxy mode selected and
.B \-\-target
set, every accepted connection is spliced to the fixed target address. This is
a straight byte forwarder with no protocol handling.
.SS SOCKS5
.RB ( \-\-as\-socks5=1 )
Implements the SOCKS5 CONNECT command (RFC 1928) for IPv4, IPv6 and
domain\-name (ATYP) targets. With
.B \-\-auth\-file
set, the username/password method (RFC 1929) is required; otherwise the
no\-authentication method is offered.
.SS HTTP
.RB ( \-\-as\-http=1 )
Handles two request styles on the same port:
.IP \(bu 3
.B CONNECT
requests, tunnelling arbitrary TCP (typically end\-to\-end TLS) to the named
host and port;
.IP \(bu 3
.B forwarding
of ordinary absolute\-form requests such as "GET http://host/path", which
gwproxy rewrites to origin form, fetches from the origin and relays back, one
request per connection.
.PP
With
.B \-\-auth\-file
set, "Basic" proxy authentication (RFC 7617) is required and a missing or wrong
credential is answered with "407 Proxy Authentication Required".
.SS Combined SOCKS5 + HTTP
Setting both
.B \-\-as\-socks5=1
and
.B \-\-as\-http=1
serves both protocols on the same listening port. The protocol is detected from
the client's first bytes, and both share the same credential store.
.SH TLS TERMINATION
When built with OpenSSL and started with
.B \-\-tls\-cert
and
.BR \-\-tls\-key ,
gwproxy can terminate a client's TLS session on its own listener (an "HTTPS
proxy"), then run the ordinary SOCKS5/HTTP logic on the decrypted stream. This
encrypts the client\(->proxy hop, protecting proxy credentials and, for cleartext
.B http://
targets, the request and URL.
.PP
TLS is applied per connection by auto\-detecting the first byte: a value of
.B 0x16
(a TLS ClientHello) starts a handshake, while SOCKS5, SOCKS4 and HTTP first
bytes do not, so
.B plaintext SOCKS5/HTTP clients keep working unchanged on the same port.
gwproxy advertises only the "http/1.1" ALPN protocol, cleanly downgrading an
HTTP/2\-preferring client. The OpenSSL engine is driven entirely through memory
BIOs and never touches the socket directly. TLS termination works on both the
epoll and io_uring event loops.
.PP
This is distinct from an HTTP
.B CONNECT
tunnel to an
.B https://
origin, which gwproxy blind\-tunnels end\-to\-end without terminating TLS, and
which needs no certificate.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
The credential file named by
.B \-\-auth\-file
holds one
.B username:password
entry per line. The same store backs SOCKS5 (RFC 1929) and HTTP "Basic" (RFC
7617) authentication; whenever it is configured, clients of either protocol
must authenticate. gwproxy watches the file and reloads it automatically when it
changes, so credentials can be updated without a restart.
.SH TRANSPARENT PROXY
.RB ( \-\-as\-transparent=1 )
Transparent mode takes each connection's original destination from the
.B SO_ORIGINAL_DST
socket option, so it must sit behind an iptables
.B REDIRECT
rule. To avoid a redirect loop, gwproxy's own outgoing connections can be marked
(with
.BR \-\-mark )
and excluded from the REDIRECT:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind=127.0.0.1:1080 \-\-as\-transparent=1 \-\-mark=1
iptables \-t nat \-A OUTPUT \-p tcp \-\-dport 80 \-m mark ! \-\-mark 1 \\
    \-j REDIRECT \-\-to\-ports 1080
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B \-\-mark
requires
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN ,
and installing the iptables rules requires the corresponding privileges;
both typically need root.
.SH UPSTREAM CHAINING
With
.BR \-\-upstream\-proxy ,
every outgoing connection is made through a next\-hop proxy rather than
directly. A
.B socks5://
upstream resolves target hostnames locally before connecting, while
.B socks5h://
defers name resolution to the upstream proxy. An
.B http://
upstream is reached with an HTTP CONNECT request, which likewise resolves the
host at the proxy. Credentials embedded in the URL authenticate to the upstream.
An
.B https://
upstream (TLS to the proxy) is not supported yet.
.SH EVENT LOOPS
Each worker uses one event loop, selected with
.BR \-\-event\-loop .
.B epoll
is always available and is the default.
.B io_uring
is available only in builds configured with io_uring support and is selected at
run time. All proxy features, including TLS termination, work on both loops.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Begin a graceful shutdown: stop accepting new connections, let workers drain,
and exit.
.TP
.B SIGPIPE
Ignored, so a peer closing a connection cannot terminate the process.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B gwproxy
exits
.B 0
on a clean shutdown and non\-zero if the configuration is invalid or a fatal
runtime error occurs.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I auth\-file
Line\-oriented
.B username:password
credential store (see
.BR \-\-auth\-file ).
.TP
.I pid\-file
Process\-ID file written when
.B \-\-pid\-file
is given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Plain TCP forwarder,
.BR [::]:8080 " \(-> " 127.0.0.1:80 :
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:8080' \-\-target=127.0.0.1:80
.EE
.RE
.PP
SOCKS5 proxy with username/password authentication:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \-\-auth\-file=auth.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
HTTP proxy (CONNECT and forwarding) on port 8080:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:8080' \-\-as\-http=1
.EE
.RE
.PP
SOCKS5 and HTTP on the same port, sharing one credential file:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \-\-as\-http=1 \-\-auth\-file=auth.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
HTTPS proxy terminating client TLS (plaintext clients still work on the same
port):
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:8080' \-\-as\-http=1 \-\-as\-socks5=1 \\
    \-\-tls\-cert=cert.pem \-\-tls\-key=key.pem
curl \-x https://127.0.0.1:8080 http://example.com/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Chain every outgoing connection through an upstream proxy (SOCKS5 or HTTP):
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \\
    \-\-upstream\-proxy=socks5h://user:pass@1.2.3.4:1080
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the io_uring event loop and set SO_MARK 100 on outgoing packets:
.RS 4
.EX
gwproxy \-\-bind='[::]:1080' \-\-as\-socks5=1 \-\-event\-loop=io_uring \-\-mark=100
.EE
.RE
.SH AVAILABILITY
Some features are enabled at build time by
.BR ./configure :
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-use\-io\-uring
builds the io_uring event loop, then selectable with
.BR \-\-event\-loop=io_uring .
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-use\-openssl
builds TLS termination; without it the
.B \-\-tls\-cert
and
.B \-\-tls\-key
options are absent.
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-use\-new\-dns\-resolver
builds the raw DNS resolver, enabling
.B \-\-raw\-dns
and
.BR \-\-dns\-server .
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-use\-pcre
builds PCRE2 regexp matches for the ACL, enabling
.B \-\-domain\-regexp
and
.BR \-\-user\-regexp ;
without it those rule options are rejected.
.PP
Options for features not compiled in are rejected. Run
.B ./configure \-\-help
for the full list.
.SH NOTES
.B \-\-mark
requires
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN .
Transparent mode requires iptables REDIRECT rules, which typically need root.
Binding to a privileged port (< 1024) requires
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
or root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR iptables (8),
.BR io_uring (7),
.BR tcp (7),
.BR socket (7),
.BR capabilities (7)
.PP
